IMPORT prelude

-- Intellectual Property Office of Singapore Act 2001 (Singapore) -- who is an intellectual property agent or adviser (s 2), the
-- Office's shield for errors in the information it supplies (s 11), misuse of the Office's symbol (s 29), officers' enforcement
-- powers and obstructing them (s 30, Third Schedule), liability of directors and partners (s 31), composition (s 33) and the
-- secrecy offence (s 36).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition ("incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on
-- 31 December 2021"), informal consolidation marked "version in force from 1/5/2024", as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/IPOSA2001.txt. The latest amendment annotated in the body is Act 22 of 2021 wef 01/05/2024
-- (Third Schedule item 5); Act 31 of 2022 wef 01/11/2022 repealed s 38 and Act 7 of 2022 wef 26/05/2022 amended ss 13 and 33. The
-- deposit's metadata records it as the current version as at 3 October 2026.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0176 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: Tier 3 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has
-- asked a sharper question yet. Most of this Act constitutes and finances a statutory board, so the row is SCOPED to the
-- provisions a member of the public or a business can meet.
--
-- Not encoded: incorporation, constitution and the First Schedule (membership, quorum, vacancy, disqualification), the Office's
-- functions and powers (ss 6, 7) beyond noting that s 6(3) makes them unenforceable in court, ministerial directions, staff, the
-- financial provisions (ss 13-20), the 2001 transfer provisions (ss 21-27), prosecution by Office officers (s 34) beyond a
-- comment, the Third Schedule's reference to rules made under s 104 of the Patents Act 1994, and rule-making (ss 35, 37). The
-- offences in the other IP Acts that the Third Schedule lists are not themselves encoded.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Intellectual Property Office of Singapore Act 2001 -- what a person or business meets`

§§ `2 -- intellectual property agents and advisers`

-- s 2: "intellectual property agent" means a person acting on behalf of another -- (a) in connection with the application for or
-- obtaining of a patent ... under the Patents Act 1994; (b) ... the registration of a trade mark ... under the Trade Marks Act
-- 1998; (c) ... the registration of a design ... under the Registered Designs Act 2000; or (d) ... the registration of a
-- geographical indication ... under the Geographical Indications Act 2014.
-- "intellectual property adviser" means any person who in the course of the person's profession or business provides legal or
-- other advice relating to ANY intellectual property, "whether in Singapore or elsewhere".
-- Copyright, which is not registered, is not in the agent definition; it is within "any intellectual property" for an adviser.
-- (Plant varieties are simply absent from the list; nothing is inferred about them beyond that.)
DECLARE Matter IS ONE OF
    `a patent application`
    `a trade mark registration`
    `a registered design`
    `a geographical indication registration`
    `a copyright dispute`
    `a plant variety application`

GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter, `acting on behalf of another` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `acting as an intellectual property agent` IF
        `acting on behalf of another`
    AND CONSIDER `the matter`
        WHEN `a patent application` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a trade mark registration` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a registered design` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a geographical indication registration` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `gives advice relating to intellectual property` IS A BOOLEAN, `in the course of a profession or business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an intellectual property adviser` IF
    `gives advice relating to intellectual property` AND `in the course of a profession or business`

§§ `11 -- protection from liability`

-- s 11(1): no suit lies PERSONALLY against any member, officer or employee "for anything which is in good faith done".
-- s 11(2): where the Office supplies information to the public "pursuant to any written law", neither the Office nor its officers
-- "shall be liable for any loss or damage suffered by any member of the public by reason of any error or omission of whatever
-- nature appearing in the information or however caused if made in good faith and in the ordinary course of the discharge of the
-- duties". (Outside the shield the Act says nothing; this rule does not decide that the Office IS liable.)
-- s 6(3): nothing in s 6 imposes on the Office "any form of duty or liability enforceable by proceedings before any court".
GIVEN `supplied to the public pursuant to written law` IS A BOOLEAN, `made in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN, `in the ordinary course of duties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 11(2) shields the Office from the error` IF
    `supplied to the public pursuant to written law` AND `made in good faith` AND `in the ordinary course of duties`

§§ `29 -- the Office's symbol`

-- s 29(1): the Office "has the exclusive right to the use of such symbol or representation as it may select or devise".
-- (2): any person who uses a symbol or representation "identical with that of the Office or which so resembles the Office's symbol
-- or representation as to deceive or cause confusion, or to be likely to deceive or cause confusion" is guilty of an offence:
-- fine not exceeding $10,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 6 months or both. An identical symbol needs no confusion.
GIVEN `uses the symbol` IS A BOOLEAN, `identical with the Office's symbol` IS A BOOLEAN, `resembles it so as to deceive or confuse, or be likely to` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `using the symbol is an offence` IF
        `uses the symbol`
    AND (`identical with the Office's symbol` OR `resembles it so as to deceive or confuse, or be likely to`)

§§ `30(1), 34 and Third Schedule -- which offences the Office's officers may enforce`

-- s 30(1): the powers apply "in relation to any offence under this Act or any written law set out in the Third Schedule".
-- Third Schedule: 1. ss 9, 10, 33, 34, 99, 100 and 101 of the Patents Act 1994 (and rules under s 104); 2. ss 51, 72 and 73 of
-- the Trade Marks Act 1998; 3. ss 29, 59, 60 and 66 of the Registered Designs Act 2000; 4. ss 25, 29 and 30 of the Geographical
-- Indications Act 2014; 5. Part 9, Division 2 of the Copyright Act 2021 [Act 22 of 2021 wef 01/05/2024].
-- s 34(1): proceedings for the same offences may, with the Public Prosecutor's authorisation, be conducted by an officer of the
-- Office authorised in writing by the Chief Executive.
DECLARE Statute IS ONE OF
    `this Act`
    `the Patents Act 1994`
    `the Trade Marks Act 1998`
    `the Registered Designs Act 2000`
    `the Geographical Indications Act 2014`
    `the Copyright Act 2021`
    `the Penal Code 1871`

GIVEN `the statute` IS A Statute, `the section` IS A NUMBER, `in Part 9 Division 2` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is within section 30` IF
    CONSIDER `the statute`
    WHEN `this Act` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Patents Act 1994` THEN
            `the section` EQUALS 9 OR `the section` EQUALS 10 OR `the section` EQUALS 33 OR `the section` EQUALS 34
         OR `the section` EQUALS 99 OR `the section` EQUALS 100 OR `the section` EQUALS 101
    WHEN `the Trade Marks Act 1998` THEN
            `the section` EQUALS 51 OR `the section` EQUALS 72 OR `the section` EQUALS 73
    WHEN `the Registered Designs Act 2000` THEN
            `the section` EQUALS 29 OR `the section` EQUALS 59 OR `the section` EQUALS 60 OR `the section` EQUALS 66
    WHEN `the Geographical Indications Act 2014` THEN
            `the section` EQUALS 25 OR `the section` EQUALS 29 OR `the section` EQUALS 30
    WHEN `the Copyright Act 2021` THEN `in Part 9 Division 2`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `30(1) -- the officers' powers`

-- s 30(1): an officer or employee of the Office may, "on declaration of his or her office and production to the person against
-- whom he or she is acting such identification card as the Chief Executive may direct" -- (a) require any person "whom he or she
-- reasonably believes to have committed that offence" to furnish evidence of identity; (b) require ANY person to furnish
-- information or produce any book or document, and inspect, copy or extract it "without fee or reward"; (c) require, "by written
-- order", the attendance of any person "within the limits of Singapore" who appears "to be acquainted with the circumstances of
-- the case".
DECLARE Power IS ONE OF
    `evidence of identity`
    `information or documents`
    `attendance`

DECLARE Demand HAS
    `the officer declared the office and produced the card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the offence is covered` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the officer reasonably believes the person committed it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the order is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person is within Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person appears acquainted with the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the power` IS A Power, `the demand` IS A Demand
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the demand is within section 30(1)` IF
        `the demand`'s `the officer declared the office and produced the card`
    AND `the demand`'s `the offence is covered`
    AND CONSIDER `the power`
        WHEN `evidence of identity` THEN `the demand`'s `the officer reasonably believes the person committed it`
        WHEN `information or documents` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `attendance` THEN
                `the demand`'s `the order is in writing`
            AND `the demand`'s `the person is within Singapore`
            AND `the demand`'s `the person appears acquainted with the circumstances`

§§ `30(2) -- obstructing an officer`

-- s 30(2): any person who -- (a) "refuses to give access to, or assaults, obstructs, hinders or delays" an officer; (b) "wilfully
-- mis-states or without lawful excuse refuses to give any information or produce any book, document"; or (c) "fails to comply with
-- a lawful demand" -- is guilty of an offence: fine not exceeding $10,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 6 months or both.
-- "Without lawful excuse" qualifies only the refusal in (b); a mis-statement is an offence only if wilful.
DECLARE Conduct HAS
    `obstructed or refused access` IS A BOOLEAN
    `wilfully mis-stated` IS A BOOLEAN
    `mis-stated by honest mistake` IS A BOOLEAN
    `refused information or documents` IS A BOOLEAN
    `had a lawful excuse for refusing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `failed to comply with a lawful demand` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conduct is an offence under section 30(2)` IF
        `the conduct`'s `obstructed or refused access`
    OR  `the conduct`'s `wilfully mis-stated`
    OR  (`the conduct`'s `refused information or documents` AND (NOT `the conduct`'s `had a lawful excuse for refusing`))
    OR  `the conduct`'s `failed to comply with a lawful demand`

§§ `31 -- directors and partners`

-- s 31(1): where an offence under this Act by a body corporate "is proved to have been committed with the consent or connivance
-- of, or to be attributable to any neglect on the part of, a director, manager, secretary or any similar officer", he or she, as
-- well as the body corporate, is guilty. (2): where members manage the affairs, a managing member is treated as a director.
-- (5): where a partnership is guilty, "every partner, other than a partner who is proved to have been ignorant of or to have
-- attempted to prevent the commission of the offence", is also guilty. The officer is caught only on proof against him or her;
-- the partner is caught unless he or she is proved ignorant or to have tried to prevent it.
GIVEN `the body corporate committed the offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `consent or connivance proved` IS A BOOLEAN, `neglect proved` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer is also guilty` IF
        `the body corporate committed the offence`
    AND (`consent or connivance proved` OR `neglect proved`)

GIVEN `the partnership is guilty` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved ignorant of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved to have tried to prevent it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the partner is also guilty` IF
        `the partnership is guilty`
    AND (NOT `proved ignorant of the offence`)
    AND (NOT `proved to have tried to prevent it`)

§§ `33 -- composition`

-- s 33(1): the Chief Executive or an authorised officer "may compound any offence under this Act that is prescribed as a
-- compoundable offence" by collecting "a sum of money not exceeding $1,000". (2): on payment, no further proceedings. (3): all
-- sums collected "must be paid into the Consolidated Fund" [Act 7 of 2022 wef 26/05/2022] -- not into the Office's funds.
GIVEN `prescribed as compoundable` IS A BOOLEAN, `the sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the composition is within section 33` IF
    `prescribed as compoundable` AND `the sum` AT MOST 1000

§§ `29(2), 30(2), 36(2) -- maximum penalties`

-- s 29(2) and s 30(2): fine not exceeding $10,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 6 months or both.
-- s 36(2): fine not exceeding $2,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 12 months or both.
-- s 32: a District Court or a Magistrate's Court may try any offence under this Act and impose the full penalty.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `misusing the Office's symbol`
    `obstructing an officer`
    `disclosing secret information`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `misusing the Office's symbol` THEN 10000
    WHEN `obstructing an officer` THEN 10000
    WHEN `disclosing secret information` THEN 2000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `misusing the Office's symbol` THEN 6
    WHEN `obstructing an officer` THEN 6
    WHEN `disclosing secret information` THEN 12

§§ `36 -- preservation of secrecy`

-- s 36(1): "Except for the purpose of the performance of his or her duties or the exercise of his or her functions or when
-- lawfully required to do so by any court or where required or allowed by the provisions of any written law", a person who "is
-- or has been a member, officer, agent, employee or former employee of the Office or a member of a committee" must not disclose
-- information "relating to the affairs of the Office or of any other person" obtained in the performance of duties.
-- (2): fine not exceeding $2,000 or imprisonment not exceeding 12 months or both.
GIVEN `is or was an insider of the Office` IS A BOOLEAN, `obtained in the course of duties` IS A BOOLEAN, `disclosed for duties` IS A BOOLEAN, `required by a court` IS A BOOLEAN, `required or allowed by written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disclosure is an offence under section 36` IF
        `is or was an insider of the Office`
    AND `obtained in the course of duties`
    AND (NOT `disclosed for duties`)
    AND (NOT `required by a court`)
    AND (NOT `required or allowed by written law`)
